Scene Two
The Question That Frightened Scientists
A silence filled the hall unlike any other silence...
It was the kind of silence that comes before the birth of lightning, when the sky holds its breath, waiting for the first flash of light.
Outside, rain poured against the windows of Dartmouth College, its drops slowly sliding down the glass as if they were trying to listen to the secret unfolding inside the hall.
But inside...
Nothing could be heard except the humming of massive computers, the sound of chalk moving across the board, and the turning of pages filled with equations, diagrams, and countless notes.
The scientists sat in a small circle, unaware that history itself was sitting among them.
The faces around the room were different...
There was the young researcher whose eyes carried endless curiosity, the professor whose hair had surrendered to years of wisdom, and the scientist who had forgotten the meaning of sleep after countless nights of research.
Yet one thing united them all...
A question that hovered above their minds like a bird searching endlessly for a place to land.
One of them slowly stood.
He was tall, holding a pencil between his fingers, turning it repeatedly without writing a single word.
He looked at his colleagues one by one, then spoke in a calm voice:
"We have taught machines how to calculate..."
He paused.
This time, silence itself became part of his speech.
Then he continued:
"And we have made them execute our commands at a speed beyond human ability."
Everyone nodded in agreement.
That was exactly what computers had always done...
They executed.
They calculated.
And they stopped.
But they...
Did not understand.
The scientist walked toward the window.
He watched the rain for a few moments, then suddenly turned around, as if a thought had struck his mind like a flash of lightning.
His voice carried a mixture of excitement and fear:
"But what if a machine could do more than calculate?"
The scientists exchanged uncertain glances.
He continued:
"What if it could learn from its own mistakes?"
The hall grew even quieter.
One researcher forgot the coffee cup in his hand, watching the steam rise from it without realizing it.
Then came the words that shattered the silence like lightning cutting through the darkness of the sky:
"Could we understand the way humans think... and then teach it to a machine?"
No one answered.
The idea was too vast to be contained in a single word.
Another scientist spoke, concern visible in his voice:
"If we succeed..."
"We may create the greatest achievement in the history of science."
Another added, clasping his hands nervously:
"And if we fail..."
"We may open a door that no one will ever be able to close."
Silence returned.
Even the rain outside seemed to soften its rhythm, as if respecting the greatness of the question that had been asked.
At the back of the hall, a distinguished elderly man watched everyone quietly.
A faint smile appeared on his face, and he said:
"Every revolution in human history began with an idea that once seemed impossible."
One of the scientists lifted a piece of chalk.
He hesitated for a moment...
As if his hand understood that the letters he was about to write would not be ordinary letters.
He approached the board.
Slowly, he drew the first letter...
Then the second...
Then the third...
Until the words appeared before the eyes of everyone:
Artificial Intelligence
A profound silence filled the room.
It was not merely a phrase...
It was a gateway.
The moment he finished writing the final letter...
Thunder exploded across the sky.
The windows shook with force.
The lights went out for a single second...
A brief second in the lifetime of the universe...
Yet it was enough to mark the beginning of a new era.
When the lights returned, the scientists looked at one another and smiled quietly.
They believed what happened was only a coincidence.
They did not know...
That the words resting peacefully on the board...
Had already crossed beyond the walls of the hall, beyond the boundaries of the city, and beyond the limits of Earth itself.
In a place unreachable by human eyes...
By maps...
Or by telescopes...
A tiny heart of blue light had begun to beat for the very first time.
One heartbeat...
That would change the destiny of the world.
